    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        If you are searching for how to get a provincial nomination in Canada, you probably already know what the Provincial Nominee Program is. (If not, start with our <a href="/articles/what-is-pnp-provincial-nominee-program" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">PNP explainer</a>, and for which province suits you, see <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">best provinces for PNP in 2027</a>.) This guide is about the doing: how provinces really choose people in 2026, the order of steps, the deadlines that catch applicants out, and a checked quick-reference table for every province and territory that nominates. It is general information, not legal advice.
      </p>

      <h2 id="four-ways" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 4 Ways Provinces Actually Select People</h2>

      <p>
        Almost no province lets you simply "apply" any more. Nearly all of them now use a two-step model: you show interest, and the province decides whom to invite. In practice, every stream falls into one or more of these four selection methods.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">1. Points-based expression of interest (EOI) pools</h3>
      <p>
        You fill in a profile, the province scores it, and it periodically invites the best-ranked people who match its current priorities. Examples: BC's Skills Immigration Registration System (scored out of 200), Manitoba's EOI with Letters of Advice to Apply, Saskatchewan's International Skilled Worker EOI, Alberta's Worker Expression of Interest, Ontario's new Ontario Workforce Priority EOI, and the EOI pools in Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Prince Edward Island and Newfoundland and Labrador. A high score is not always enough. Alberta says its EOI score "is not the only factor" it uses, and Nova Scotia says selections are based on priorities and are not first-come, first-served.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">2. Express Entry-linked selections</h3>
      <p>
        If you have an Express Entry profile, IRCC says there are two ways to be nominated: apply to a province's Express Entry stream yourself, or mark your interest in provinces in your profile so they can find you. A province that likes your profile may send a <strong>notification of interest</strong> to your IRCC account. IRCC is clear that this "is not a nomination and does not guarantee you'll be nominated"; you still have to apply to the province. Nova Scotia, for example, directs people who received a Notice of Interest to its Critical Vacancies page. Most provinces' Express Entry streams also require you to go through their own EOI first.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">3. Employer and job-offer-driven streams</h3>
      <p>
        Here the job offer is the gate. In Yukon, an employer submits the EOI during set intake windows. In the Northwest Territories, the employer registers first and the candidate then completes an EOI survey. Saskatchewan employers must have the position approved through an Employer Position Assessment before a worker can apply. BC, Ontario and Newfoundland and Labrador require a qualifying job offer for every worker stream.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">4. In-province graduates and workers</h3>
      <p>
        Provinces increasingly favour people already there. Manitoba's October 8, 2026 draw invited only candidates declaring current employment in Manitoba in health or education roles, plus those directly recruited by the province. Alberta's Opportunity Stream requires that you are working in Alberta on a valid work permit. Saskatchewan reserves 750 priority-sector spaces for Saskatchewan post-secondary graduates, and Newfoundland and Labrador's published criteria prioritize graduates with ties to the province. BC no longer has graduate streams, but Canadian education still earns registration points.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">What this means for you</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">Before choosing a province, ask which of these four doors you can realistically walk through. If you have no job offer, no connection to the province and no Express Entry profile, most 2026 streams are closed to you, whatever your points.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="base-enhanced" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Base vs Enhanced Nominations: What Each Means for PR</h2>

      <p>
        Every nomination ends with a federal application to IRCC, and there are two routes. The province or territory tells you which one applies to you.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">&nbsp;</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Enhanced (Express Entry)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Base (non-Express Entry)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Who it is for</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You qualify for a provincial stream and a federal Express Entry program (Federal Skilled Worker, Canadian Experience Class or Federal Skilled Trades)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You qualify for the provincial stream but not for an Express Entry program, for example many TEER 4 and 5 workers</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">What happens after nomination</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Accept the nomination in your Express Entry profile within 30 calendar days; 600 CRS points are added; wait for an invitation in a PNP draw; apply online</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Submit a non-Express Entry permanent residence application to IRCC with your nomination certificate</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Speed</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Usually faster; IRCC aims to process most complete Express Entry applications within six months</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Usually slower; check IRCC's current processing times tool</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Main risk</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Your Express Entry profile must stay valid and eligible, including language test dates</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You must apply before the nomination expires and keep meeting its conditions</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Express Entry changes from November 3, 2026</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">On October 16, 2026, IRCC announced Express Entry changes taking effect November 3, 2026. Candidates with Canadian work experience or a qualifying job offer in a high-wage occupation (one whose median wage is at least 1.3 times the Canadian median) will receive additional CRS points, and a qualifying job offer under the Federal Skilled Worker and Federal Skilled Trades programs will no longer need an LMIA. Profiles in the pool are rescored automatically and draws may pause for up to two weeks during the switch. The announcement does not mention any change to the 600 points for a provincial nomination.</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        IRCC's federal fee is the same either way: $1,590 for a principal applicant (a $990 processing fee plus the $600 right of permanent residence fee), the same for a spouse, and $270 per dependent child, as listed on its fee page in October 2026. See our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-application-fees-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">PR fees guide</a> for a full budget.
      </p>

      <h2 id="steps" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Get Nominated by a Province in Canada: 8 Steps</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-4">
        <li><strong>Choose the province by your profile, not its allocation.</strong> Start from where you work or have a real job offer, your occupation's NOC code and TEER level, and whether you are already in Canada. A smaller program that targets your occupation beats a large one that does not. Our <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">province comparison</a> matches common profiles to programs.</li>
        <li><strong>Check one stream's eligibility line by line.</strong> Read the stream page on the official provincial site, not a summary. Look for the job offer rules, minimum work experience, language level, education, work permit type (Alberta's Opportunity Stream, for example, does not accept maintained or restoration status) and any excluded occupations or sectors.</li>
        <li><strong>Get your language test.</strong> Book IELTS General Training, CELPIP-General or PTE Core for English, or TEF Canada or TCF Canada for French, after checking which tests your province's stream accepts. Results are generally accepted for two years, so time the test so it is still valid when you apply to IRCC. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-language-requirements" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">language guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Get an Educational Credential Assessment (ECA) for foreign education.</strong> Express Entry needs one for foreign credentials, and many provincial scoring grids only count education you can prove. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-eca-guide" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">ECA guide</a> explains which organization to use.</li>
        <li><strong>Secure and check the job offer.</strong> For most streams it must be full-time, from an employer that meets provincial rules, and in an eligible occupation, with a wage in line with the local rate. Confirm the employer is real before you register (see the fraud section below).</li>
        <li><strong>Register your EOI or profile, and keep it accurate.</strong> Some provinces charge for this (Alberta has charged $135 per Worker EOI since April 7, 2026); BC registration is free. Profiles expire: BC removes a registration after 12 months without an invitation, Alberta Worker EOIs are valid for one year, and Nova Scotia introduced a 12-month EOI validity period on May 1, 2026. If you hold an Express Entry profile, select the provinces you are interested in.</li>
        <li><strong>Respond to the invitation fast.</strong> Deadlines are short and usually cannot be extended: 30 calendar days in BC, 7 calendar days for Nova Scotia to update documents and pay, and 15 days for Alberta invitations. Have your documents ready before you are invited. Then submit the provincial application and pay the fee through the official portal only.</li>
        <li><strong>Receive your nomination and apply to IRCC on time.</strong> If approved, you receive a nomination certificate (and, in some provinces, a work permit support letter). Accept it in Express Entry within 30 days, or file your base application before the expiry date shown on the certificate.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="table" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Province-by-Province Quick Reference (October 2026)</h2>

      <p>
        We checked each entry against the program's official pages on October 18, 2026 and only list what we could confirm. "Job offer" refers to most worker streams; entrepreneur streams are excluded.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-3 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Province / territory</th>
              <th class="px-3 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Main selection method</th>
